the iphone 14 is gonna be better than a  lot of the people think it's gonna be at  least according to the current sentiment  that's going around with a lot of  different reports saying that the lower  end models of this year's iphone 14  lineup are going to be very  disappointing with very little changes  compared to the iphone 13. for example  we're not expecting any big changes in  the camera department other than an  upgrade to the selfie camera but on the  pro models they're going to be getting a  brand new 48 megapixel main camera that  supports 8k video recording at 60fps on  top of that the pro models will likely  be getting an always on display mode and  a new dual hole punch selfie camera  design compared to the current notch  design that's going to the regular  models and going even further it looks  like only the pro models will be getting  the a16 chip this year with the regular  iphone 14 and 14 max models sticking  with the previous a15 chip that we  already have in the current iphone 13.  and we just got a new report from the  elec which mentions that the new pro  models will be getting higher quality  m12 graded materials for the display  panels compared to the regular iphone 14  models which will have lower m11  standards which is a move to help apple  cut costs so because of those reasons  and a few others a lot of people are  expecting the regular iphone 14s to be a  crappy and disappointing upgrade but in  this video i'm gonna talk about some  reasons why i think the regular iphone  14 models will actually be pretty solid  in terms of upgrades compared to the  iphone 13 lineup including shrimp apple  pro saying that there will be a  performance improvement even despite  sticking with the same a15 chip but  before i get into those brand new leaks  i want to discuss the whole a15 versus a  16 chip ordeal and why i think it's not  going to be that big of an issue this  time around according to shrimp apple  pro who has proven himself to be pretty  reliable in terms of leaks like breaking  the iphone 14 pro dual hole punch design  way back in july of last year and he has  also said back in may that the a16  bionic chip coming this year will  continue to use this same five nanometer  tsmc n5p process node that the a15 chip  is based on right now yes the exact same  process now a lot of people will  probably be asking how could that  possibly make any sense at all because  the android phones that we have right  now especially last year's snapdragon 8  gen 1 it's built on the samsung 4  nanometer process as well as the tsmc 4  nanometer process which is basically n4p  so why would apple stick to 5 nanometer  with n5p  well first of all it seems like 4  nanometer didn't really make much of a  difference in the first place because if  you look at the benchmarks or the tests  from geekerwan on the snapdragon 8gen 1  they basically called it a piece of crap  with the cherry on top because if you  look at this you have the snapdragon 88  right here that's the old version and if  you look at the score per watt it beaded  out  basically in single core and multi-core  with better score per watt so it was  actually a downgrade in terms of the  efficiency so it seems like the whole  four nanometer thing was just a  marketing stunt or basically trying to  sell it however the new snapdragon 8 gen  1 plus is finally seeing some decent  efficiency gains with some really  impressive efficiency this time around  and that seems like it's because they  are now fine tuning the design and  properly optimizing it so with that said  what if apple could get the same amount  of gains just with a new design and more  optimizations on the same n5p 5  nanometer process now as for another  reason why apple chose to stick with n5p  what if apple planned to make the a16  chip on three nanometer but it was  delayed  so that could have been a cause but  honestly i do not think it's a big deal  that they're sticking to n5p and that's  mainly because of the overheating  problems that the iphone has had for a  couple of years ago when it was very  very bad on the iphone 12 lineup when  they introduced 5g and it did get better  with the iphone 13 series but the issue  is still hanging around so basically the  chip doesn't have a good way of cooling  itself down with it just having the most  basic form of passive cooling and we  even showed you guys that in terms of  performance per gpu core the new 4 core  version of the a15 chip isn't much  different than the four core version of  the previous a14 for example the ipad  air 4 with the a14 chip scores 10.6  higher than the iphone 13 with the new  a15 chip in terms of geekbench 5 metal  graphics performance so the point that  i'm trying to make is that with the new  a16 chip apple can raise the clock speed  that the chip runs at to make it faster  but the problem with that is that the  current a15 chip is already vastly  limited because of the overheating  issues so making the a16 chip faster  isn't going to do much at all for the  performance however if apple were to  dramatically improve the passive cooling  system itself it would essentially  unlock a lot of lost performance that  they were missing out on because of the  excessive overheating and that's not  even a joke just by fixing the passive  cooling problems even the old a14 chip  within the new iphone 14 would instantly  outperform the a15 chip within the  iphone 13. and luckily we've been  hearing some rumors from the well-known  apple analyst meets jaquo that apple is  aggressively testing new vapor chamber  thermal systems for use in the iphone  suggesting that the technology will make  its way to the flagship iphone in the  near future and to add further evidence  for why it should be ready shrimp apple  pro mentioned that this year's iphone 14  lineup should come with a new internal  design which basically means a  redesigned logic board compared to the  iphone 13 lineup so why not add in a  vapor chamber cooler right now while  they're at it and that basically makes  apple's job a lot easier with this  year's iphones because they can easily  reuse the same old n5p process node from  tsmc without having to make major  changes to the a16 chip and still get a  really nice performance boost simply  because of the new cooling system  allowing apple to unlock that lost  performance from overheating so because  of that i think shrimp apple pro is  totally right about apple reusing n5p  again because it's going to be the  cheapest possible solution for apple  which is important in these inflationary  times and the end consumer won't know  any better because the a16 ship will in  fact be faster than before and miles  ahead of the competition and not only  that but consumer satisfaction will go  up with the new iphone 14 lineup because  we likely won't have to deal with those  display dimming issues anymore at least  nowhere near as bad as it currently is  and then next year with the a17 chip  they can finally move on to tsmc's three  nanometer process node which will be a  lot more efficient and then as far as  the regular iphone 14 model sticking to  the same a15 chip while the pro models  get the a16 well shrimp apple pro  believes that despite sticking to the  same ship the regular non-pro models  will still have an overall performance  boost over the iphone 13 series and that  totally makes sense especially if apple  is going to be upgrading the cooling  system which will instantly give it a  performance boost with the same exact  chip and on top of that rumors are  pointing to the entire iphone 14 lineup  coming with six gigs of ram this time  around compared to the regular iphone 13  models that come with only four  gigabytes right now so that by itself is  going to be a nice upgrade for people  who choose to stick with the regular  iphone 14 models so for all those  reasons that i mentioned i honestly  think that the iphone 14 is still going  to be a pretty good upgrade and it's  going to be quite a bit better than the  iphone 13 models that we have right now  even despite sticking with the a15 chip  and not getting some of the other pro  features i think it's still going to be  a nice worthy upgrade now if you totally  disagree with me let me know in the  comment section below but if you enjoyed  this video and learn something new click  the circle above to subscribe for more  videos like this one and check out one  of those two right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]
 

